The atomists: explaining change through what persists (@greek-atomists: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-nature-being, #source @greek-source-burnet, #philosophy @greek-comparative-inquiry, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-comparative-inquiry, #philosophy @greek-epicurus, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-epicurus, #philosophy @greek-plato-aristotle, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-plato-aristotle) { r_666ZSW8DNDQ45TJKB8K9HKVS5B
**Read:** [Early Greek Philosophy](../sources/Early-Greek-Philosophy-Burnet.lingua#r_1DSKX3DB6E343PZHZABGATVK13), Chapter IX, “Leukippos of Miletos”, including its treatment of atoms, void, and the relation to Democritus. Return to relevant passages on change and not-being in the preceding readings.

**Portuguese alternative:** [Os pré-socráticos: fragmentos, doxografia e comentários, Nova Cultural](../sources/Presocratics-Nova-Cultural.lingua#r_6ZA1ENV6PB59E6EGC7MCBS1782). Use the corresponding thinker's section, keeping fragments, ancient reports, and commentary distinct. The guide's question can be investigated in either collection; identify the edition and passage when comparing them.

**Preparation:** distinguish an explanatory principle from a directly observed object. [Parmenides](Parmenides.lingua#r_7HVRAJY7AF3178NEH1N8BF9NPV) helps explain the problem the account is responding to; read its reconstruction alongside if needed.

The atomist account connects change in arrangements with the persistence of its proposed constituents. Ask what the void explains, why the constituents have the properties assigned to them, and how the account relates appearances to a less immediately visible structure.

**Practice:** compare a change of arrangement with a change in a thing's constituent nature. State the explanatory benefit of the distinction and an assumption that would still need justification. Ancient atoms and the entities in modern atomic physics belong to different theories; identify what a comparison shares before drawing a conclusion.

**Check:** explain how the account responds to a problem about change without claiming that later technology verifies every part of the ancient doctrine.

**Continue:** [Epicurus on nature, desire, and living well](../schools/Epicurus.lingua#r_6KHZKEFRKY1J18Z7TSRAJABZQP), or [a comparative investigation](../questions/Comparative-investigation.lingua#r_20BK69Q76HJXAGZ5J1T097X29G). **Return:** [Nature and being](Nature-and-being.lingua#r_61M87EN28QQ9AHXT80SPCB0CVH) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
